ORGO.reg({"chapters":{"rings-1":{"id":"rings-1","n":1,"title":"Rings and ring strain","lecture":"2026-10-20","from":"Lecture notes 10/20, pages 1 to 3","goals":["Say where ring strain comes from"],"sections":[{"id":"cyclopropane","title":"Cyclopropane: angle strain and bent bonds","pages":["L1020:1"],"html":"<p>A tetrahedral carbon wants bond angles of 109.5°. In cyclopropane the ring forces 60°. That squeeze is <b>angle strain</b>.</p>\n[[mol:C1CC1]]\n<p>The orbitals cannot point along the edges of the triangle, so they overlap off to the side and make <b>bent bonds</b>: less overlap, a weaker bond. C-C in propane 88 kcal/mol, in cyclopropane 65.</p>","micro":[{"t":"Why less overlap means a weaker bond","h":"<p>A sigma bond is strongest when the two orbitals point straight at each other.</p>"}],"macro":[{"t":"Why this matters later","h":"<p>Strained rings open easily. The epoxide in the past exam's Question 4B is opened by methoxide for exactly this reason.</p>"}],"why":{"q":"Why is the C-C bond of cyclopropane weaker than that of propane?","a":"Its orbitals overlap off-axis (bent bonds), so there is less overlap."},"check":[]},{"id":"torsional","title":"Torsional strain: eclipsed bonds","pages":["L1020:1"],"html":"<p>Look down any C-C bond of cyclopropane and every C-H bond is <b>eclipsed</b>: six eclipsing interactions. That is <b>torsional strain</b>. Ring strain = angle strain + torsional strain.</p>\n[[newman:H,H,C|H,H,C|0]]","check":[]}],"summary":["Ring strain is angle strain plus torsional strain."]}},"mols":{"C1CC1":{"w":32,"h":37,"p":[[0,"M 27.8,18.4 L 3.8,4.6",0],[1,"M 3.8,4.6 L 3.8,32.3",0],[2,"M 3.8,32.3 L 27.8,18.4",0],[-1,"M 26.6,17.7 L 27.8,18.4 L 26.6,19.1",0],[-1,"M 5.0,5.3 L 3.8,4.6 L 3.8,6.0",0],[-1,"M 3.8,30.9 L 3.8,32.3 L 5.0,31.6",0]],"t":[],"a":[[27.8,18.4,"C",0,0,2,0],[3.8,4.6,"C",0,0,2,0],[3.8,32.3,"C",0,0,2,0]],"b":[[0,1,1],[1,2,1],[2,0,1]]}}});
